latest_plan = "Based on the refined understanding of the challenge, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve it:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid to avoid modifying the original.\n\n2. Create a boolean matrix of the same size as the grid to mark reachable cells. Initialize all cells as False.\n\n3. Implement a flood fill function:\n   - This function should take the grid, the boolean matrix, and starting coordinates (row, col) as parameters.\n   - It should use a queue or stack for a breadth-first or depth-first search.\n   - The function should mark cells as reachable (True in the boolean matrix) if:\n     a) They are within the grid boundaries\n     b) They are not blue (value != 1)\n     c) They haven't been marked as reachable yet\n\n4. Iterate through all edge cells of the grid:\n   - For each non-blue edge cell, call the flood fill function with its coordinates.\n   - This will mark all cells reachable from non-blue edges.\n\n5. Iterate through the entire grid:\n   - If a cell is not marked as reachable (False in the boolean matrix) and is not blue (value != 1), change it to red (2).\n   - Otherwise, leave it unchanged.\n\n6. Return the modified grid.\n\nHere's a more detailed breakdown of the implementation:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve_292dd178(input_grid: ColoredGrid) -> ColoredGrid:\n    # Step 1: Create a deep copy of the input grid\n    grid = input_grid.deep_copy()\n    rows, cols = grid.get_dimensions()\n\n    # Step 2: Create a boolean matrix to mark reachable cells\n    reachable = [[False for _ in range(cols)] for _ in range(rows)]\n\n    # Step 3: Implement flood fill function\n    def flood_fill(start_r: int, start_c: int):\n        queue = deque([(start_r, start_c)])\n        while queue:\n            r, c = queue.popleft()\n            if 0 <= r < rows and 0 <= c < cols and grid.values[r][c] != 1 and not reachable[r][c]:\n                reachable[r][c] = True\n                for dr, dc in [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]:\n                    queue.append((r + dr, c + dc))\n\n    # Step 4: Flood fill from non-blue edge cells\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if (r == 0 or r == rows - 1 or c == 0 or c == cols - 1) and grid.values[r][c] != 1:\n                flood_fill(r, c)\n\n    # Step 5: Fill unreachable, non-blue cells with red\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if not reachable[r][c] and grid.values[r][c] != 1:\n                grid.values[r][c] = 2\n\n    # Step 6: Return the modified grid\n    return grid\n```\n\nThis implementation should correctly handle all the cases we've discussed:\n- It preserves blue cells.\n- It only fills cells with red if they can't be reached from a non-blue edge without crossing blue cells.\n- It handles cases where red areas extend to the grid edges, even if not fully enclosed by blue cells.\n- It works for multiple enclosed areas and nested structures within the same grid.\n\nThis solution applies the same logic consistently across all cases, addressing the nuances we observed in our reflection."
